---
import CourseLesson from "../../../layouts/CourseLesson.astro";
import Math from "../../../components/course/Math.astro";
import Callout from "../../../components/course/Callout.astro";
import Quiz from "../../../components/course/Quiz.astro";
import VennExplorer from "../../../components/course/widgets/VennExplorer.astro";
---

<CourseLesson course="probability" lessonSlug="axioms-and-inclusion-exclusion">
  <h2>Three axioms</h2>
  <p>
    A probability is a function <Math tex="P(\cdot)" /> that assigns a number to each event, subject
    to exactly three rules. Everything else in this course is a consequence of them.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Non-negativity.</strong> <Math tex="P(A) \ge 0" /> for every event <Math tex="A" />.</li>
    <li><strong>Normalization.</strong> <Math tex="P(S) = 1" /> &mdash; something happens.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Additivity.</strong> If <Math tex="A \cap B = \varnothing" />, then{" "}
      <Math tex="P(A \cup B) = P(A) + P(B)" />.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Notice how narrow the third one is. It says nothing at all about overlapping events &mdash; and
    that restriction is what the rest of this lesson is about.
  </p>

  <h3>Immediate consequences</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <Math tex="P(A^c) = 1 - P(A)" />, because <Math tex="A" /> and <Math tex="A^c" /> are disjoint
      and their union is <Math tex="S" />.
    </li>
    <li><Math tex="P(\varnothing) = 0" />, taking <Math tex="A = S" /> above.</li>
    <li>
      <Math tex="0 \le P(A) \le 1" />, and if <Math tex="A \subseteq B" /> then{" "}
      <Math tex="P(A) \le P(B)" />.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why the additive rule breaks</h2>
  <p>
    If <Math tex="A" /> and <Math tex="B" /> overlap and you write <Math tex="P(A) + P(B)" />, the
    outcomes in <Math tex="A \cap B" /> get counted twice &mdash; once as members of{" "}
    <Math tex="A" />, once as members of <Math tex="B" />. The repair is to subtract the double
    count:
  </p>
  <Math display tex="P(A \cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \cap B)" />
  <p>For three events the pattern continues, alternating signs:</p>
  <Math
    display
    tex="P(A \cup B \cup C) = P(A) + P(B) + P(C) - P(A \cap B) - P(A \cap C) - P(B \cap C) + P(A \cap B \cap C)"
  />
  <p>
    That is <strong>inclusion&ndash;exclusion</strong>: add the singles, subtract the pairs, add the
    triple back. The triple intersection is removed three times by the pair terms after being added
    three times by the singles, so it needs one addition to survive.
  </p>

  <h2>The same answer without a single subtraction</h2>
  <p>
    Inclusion&ndash;exclusion is a repair job: over-count, then correct. There is a cleaner route
    that never over-counts in the first place &mdash; <strong>disjointify</strong>. Break the union
    into non-overlapping pieces and use axiom 3 directly, which is the only additive rule you
    actually have.
  </p>
  <p>
    From the previous lesson, the three circles cut <Math tex="S" /> into eight disjoint regions.
    Seven of them make up <Math tex="A \cup B \cup C" />, so:
  </p>
  <Math
    display
    tex="\begin{aligned} P(A \cup B \cup C) = \;& P(A \cap B^c \cap C^c) + P(A^c \cap B \cap C^c) + P(A^c \cap B^c \cap C) \\ &+ P(A \cap B \cap C^c) + P(A \cap B^c \cap C) + P(A^c \cap B \cap C) \\ &+ P(A \cap B \cap C) \end{aligned}"
  />
  <p>
    Every term is a probability of a region no other term touches, so the sum is exact &mdash; no
    corrections needed. A more compact version of the same idea peels the events off one at a time:
  </p>
  <Math
    display
    tex="P(A \cup B \cup C) = P(A) + P(A^c \cap B) + P(A^c \cap B^c \cap C)"
  />
  <p>
    Read it as: take all of <Math tex="A" />; then whatever of <Math tex="B" /> is new; then whatever
    of <Math tex="C" /> is new after both. Three disjoint slices, three additions, no subtraction.
  </p>

  <Callout variant="intuition" label="Which form should you use?">
    <p>
      Use inclusion&ndash;exclusion when the problem hands you the pairwise and triple
      intersections. Use the disjoint form when it hands you the atoms directly &mdash; or when it
      forbids subtraction, which is really a way of forcing you to prove you understand{" "}
      <em>why</em> the subtraction was needed.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p>
    Shade "at least 1 event occurred" in the explorer below and count: seven regions, one per term
    in the expansion above.
  </p>

  <VennExplorer />

  <h2>The complement shortcut</h2>
  <p>
    For a union of many events, the eighth region is often the fastest path of all:
  </p>
  <Math display tex="P(A \cup B \cup C) = 1 - P(A^c \cap B^c \cap C^c)" />
  <p>
    If the events are also independent &mdash; a notion the next lesson makes precise &mdash; that
    last term factors into <Math tex="\bigl(1-P(A)\bigr)\bigl(1-P(B)\bigr)\bigl(1-P(C)\bigr)" />, and
    a messy seven-term expansion collapses to one line. This is exactly the move behind parallel
    reliability blocks later in the course.
  </p>

  <Quiz
    question="P(A) = 0.5, P(B) = 0.4, P(A ∩ B) = 0.3. What is P(A ∪ B)?"
    options={["0.9", "0.7", "0.6", "0.2"]}
    correctIndex={2}
    correctText="Correct: 0.5 + 0.4 − 0.3 = 0.6. The overlap was counted in both marginals, so it comes off once."
    incorrectText="Adding 0.5 + 0.4 counts the 0.3 overlap twice, so subtract it once: 0.5 + 0.4 − 0.3 = 0.6."
  />
</CourseLesson>
